From f85895dfa2abcd19fced3b6de41e6f78e755e5b0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 14:21:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 04/10] S2-10 PS1: a live screen over the save, the load and
 the build (loading screen per citizen, card calls two sectors at a time, a
 spinner over the overlay reads)

- plat_busy / plat_busy_step (gfx.c): the full-screen loading screen is redrawn with its bar at done / total, at most
  every other vblank and only once the work has run three vblanks (a quick job shows nothing), over save_game's
  encode, load_game's decode, town_new, rel_normalize's passes and sim_init.
- memcard.c: card writes and reads (the loading screen is up) go two 128-byte sectors per BIOS call with a picture
  after each call instead of one 8 KB block per call (the bar moved once a second): about the old total time
  (two sectors a call keeps the BIOS's per-call cost; one sector a call was 34% slower), pictures every 70-100 ms
  in PCSX-Redux. A peek (the 48-byte header a menu asks for, the config record) reads the 6-7 frames it needs, not
  the 64 of a whole block: the 2.3 s the Save/Load screen's refresh took after a save is about 10x less. The line
  under the title says "Writing..." / "Reading..." (the "block N of M" text went: 400 B of resident code).
- gfx_load_fade: once the three darkening passes are done and the drive is still reading (a scene change loads up to
  three groups: a New story reads seven, 2.4 s), a ring of eight dots turns on the dark picture every other vblank.
- The harness counts the pictures the game shows outside its loop (g_busy_pics: fade passes, spinner steps, loading
  screens) with the loop's frames (run.py HARNESS_PICS), so the gaps line sees a live screen.
- Resident RAM: the banner's and the loading screen's glyph arrays are one (800 B of BSS back); the cold busy code is
  compiled -Os by attribute. `_end` 0x801efe60 -> 0x801efd20: 320 B LESS resident than before S2-10, 64 KB below the
  stack top (the margin unchanged at the page).
- rel_normalize steps once per pass (not per 64 edges: the inner loop's step cost 108 B of code).

Same bytes: the 128 KB memory card image of a deterministic island save (seed 1, tick-exact script) is
sha256-identical between the old and new disc; memcard_check reads it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
